<!--{ pr01 : rich }-->
# 12. Your first project

The last lesson of the core path: a folder of your own, for your own
documents. This lesson has no checks, since your project is outside the
tutorial. About five minutes.
<!--{ pr02 : rich }-->
## Make a project

A **project** is a folder of documents that belong together, such as the
calculations for one structure, with the libraries they use. Everything it
needs is inside its folder, so it works as a whole wherever you copy it: to
a colleague, to a server, into a backup.

In the second terminal, make one, for example in a folder `first-project`
of your home folder:

```
cd ~
stilo init first-project
```

`stilo init` creates the folder if needed, writes `project.texl`, which marks
it as a project, and copies the Stilo library into `lib/stilo`, which data
tables need (lesson 7). On Windows, write the folder as you usually do, as
`stilo init C:\Users\you\first-project`.
<!--{ pr03 : rich }-->
## Serve it

`stilo serve` opens a folder in the editor, as `stilo tutor` opens this
tutorial. The tutorial already uses the port 8000, so give your project
another one:

```
stilo serve first-project --port 8001
```

Open `http://127.0.0.1:8001/` in your browser, press **Alt+N** for a new
document, and write. Everything from the lessons works there. Press **Ctrl+C**
in the terminal to stop it.

Next time, stop the tutorial first and run `stilo serve first-project`,
which uses the port 8000.
<!--{ pr04 : rich }-->
## Libraries

In an import, `~` stands for the project folder, so every document of the
project imports the Stilo library as `~/lib/stilo/index.texl`, however deep
in its folders it is. Your own libraries go in `lib` too: copy the folder
`lib/steel` of this tutorial into your project, and every document of it
can import `~/lib/steel/sections.texl`, as in lesson 8.

## Serving to others

`stilo serve` has no accounts and no passwords: anybody who can reach it can
change every document of the folder. By default it accepts connections from
your own computer only. Keep it that way, unless you put your own access
control in front of it.

More in the manual: [Projects and libraries](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/editor/projects.html),
[stilo init](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/cli/init.html) and
[stilo serve](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/cli/serve.html).
<!--{ pr05 : rich }-->
## Well done

You have finished the core path. The [index](index.texl) shows your checks.
Two advanced lessons follow, for when you want more: the language of the
formulas, and your own libraries.

